# Who can open the household's copy — ADR-0073.
#
# Every Immich library set and every Paperless-ngx document set is encrypted to
# these keys as well as to the sensitive rule's, so the copy on the household
# drive opens without the operator. These are PUBLIC keys: they can only
# encrypt. This file opens nothing in secrets/, and scripts/check_sops_rules.py
# fails if a household key ever appears in .sops.yaml.
#
# The comment directly above each key gives its role, and there are three:
#
#   household         the person who holds the drive at the other address and
#                     opens it from their own device (ADR-0023). Generated ON
#                     that device, by them; only the age1 line comes here.
#   technical-second  ADR-0024's second recipient, the fallback if the
#                     household key is lost. The same key as the catch-all
#                     rule's second recipient in .sops.yaml.
#   household-and-technical-second
#                     one person who is both: their single key, written once.
#                     It stays in the catch-all rule, as the technical second.
#
# A fallback is required: a file with a household key and no technical-second
# (or combined) key is refused.
#
# There is no household key yet: the holder is not chosen (#455). Until there
# is, no copy on the drive is recorded as the household's, because a copy that
# only the operator and the technician can open is not the path ADR-0023 asks
# for. The holder generates their key on their own device and sends the age1
# line; it goes here, above the technical second, under `# role: household`.
#
# Read with scripts/household-recipients.sh, never by eye; it refuses a key
# with no role, a role with no key and a line that is not a key. The file is
# also valid input to `age -R` as it stands.

# role: technical-second  (ADR-0024, #294)
age19mkg76v0x70wkqwuykxxqpdwrq8mhgklpjw44j6xw73psnhwyvcqa9995j
